<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shinran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1173">1173</a>–<a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shinran was a Japanese Buddhist monk, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Hōnen</a> and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a>, or True Pure Land school. Exiled in 1207, he married and lived among ordinary people, teaching that salvation depended entirely on faith in the Buddha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>. His school later became the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a> leagues.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinran.jpg" alt="Shinran" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanbokucho-period artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinran_(Nara_National_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Teachers in later centuries added their own classics, among them the Japanese monks <b>Shinran</b> and Dogen and the Tibetan scholar Tsongkhapa, founder of the Gelug school.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>New Buddhist movements of Honen, <b>Shinran</b>, Nichiren and Zen spread widely.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hino Tanjō-in Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.93,135.82&amp;year=1231" aria-label="Hino Tanjō-in Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7994 900 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shinran"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8444 1170h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8444 1170h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.93,135.82&amp;year=1231">What was here in 1231? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the ignorant exile who sinks into the deeps of the great ocean of human affections, who toils to climb the high mountains of worldly prosperity…&#8221;</p><footer>A lament from his writings (in Buddhist Psalms, introduction) &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Difficult is it for men to find a wise Teacher; so is it also for them to be instructed and to hear the Holy Law. More difficult still is it to receive the True Faith.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 67 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Go forward, O Valiant Souls, seeking the Law though all the worlds fall into flame and ruin, for ye shall have passed beyond birth and death!&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 29 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sin is made one with virtue in its essence, even as ice is one with water. The more there is ice, so much the more water is there.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 156 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When every man is beloved of us, even as the son of our own body, there is the Universal Mind made perfect in us.&#8221;</p><footer>Wasan (hymns), 90 &#183; tr. S. Yamabe and L. Adams Beck, 1921 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7015/pg7015-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
